 𝗗𝗮𝘆 𝟮𝟮/𝟯𝟬 – 𝗔𝗻𝘀𝗶𝗯𝗹𝗲 𝗜𝗻𝘃𝗲𝗻𝘁𝗼𝗿𝘆 & 𝗗𝘆𝗻𝗮𝗺𝗶𝗰 𝗜𝗻𝘃𝗲𝗻𝘁𝗼𝗿𝘆

Continuing my hashtag#30DaysOfAnsible journey! 🔥

Today I focused on one of the most important building blocks of Ansible — Inventory.

🎯 𝗪𝗵𝗮𝘁 𝗶𝘀 𝗔𝗻𝘀𝗶𝗯𝗹𝗲 𝗜𝗻𝘃𝗲𝗻𝘁𝗼𝗿𝘆?

Ansible Inventory tells Ansible which machines to manage and how they are organized.

A simple inventory can look like:

[web]
web01
web02

[db]
db01
db02

Now we can target the web servers:

ansible web -m ping

Or run a playbook against them:

- hosts: web
 tasks:
 - name: Install Nginx
 ansible.builtin.apt:
 name: nginx
 state: present

🔄 𝗦𝘁𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗰 𝘃𝘀 𝗗𝘆𝗻𝗮𝗺𝗶𝗰 𝗜𝗻𝘃𝗲𝗻𝘁𝗼𝗿𝘆

📌 𝗦𝘁𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗰 𝗜𝗻𝘃𝗲𝗻𝘁𝗼𝗿𝘆

Hosts are manually defined in an inventory file.

Best for: ✔️ Small environments
✔️ Stable infrastructure
✔️ Simple setups

📌 𝗗𝘆𝗻𝗮𝗺𝗶𝗰 𝗜𝗻𝘃𝗲𝗻𝘁𝗼𝗿𝘆

Hosts are discovered automatically from infrastructure platforms.

This is especially useful in cloud environments where servers are constantly:

➕ Created
➖ Terminated
🔄 Scaled

☁️ 𝗥𝗲𝗮𝗹-𝗪𝗼𝗿𝗹𝗱 𝗗𝗲𝘃𝗢𝗽𝘀 𝗘𝘅𝗮𝗺𝗽𝗹𝗲

Imagine an AWS environment with:

🌐 Web Servers
⚙️ Application Servers
🗄️ Database Servers

Maintaining every server manually becomes difficult as the infrastructure grows.

With Dynamic Inventory, Ansible can automatically discover the current infrastructure and manage the appropriate hosts.

💡 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴

𝗜𝗻𝘃𝗲𝗻𝘁𝗼𝗿𝘆 = 𝗧𝗵𝗲 𝗯𝗿𝗶𝗱𝗴𝗲 𝗯𝗲𝘁𝘄𝗲𝗲𝗻 𝗔𝗻𝘀𝗶𝗯𝗹𝗲 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝘆𝗼𝘂𝗿 𝗶𝗻𝗳𝗿𝗮𝘀𝘁𝗿𝘂𝗰𝘁𝘂𝗿𝗲.

Static Inventory → Simple & predictable
Dynamic Inventory → Scalable & automated
